18:47.700 --> 18:53.180
the bill fundamentally unfair, while citizen B is passionately supportive, declaring it the

18:53.180 --> 18:54.200
embodiment of fairness.

18:54.620 --> 19:00.200
In standard political discourse, what happens next? Citizen A assumes citizen B is brainwashed,

19:00.360 --> 19:05.980
or stupid, or acting in bad faith. Citizen B assumes the exact same about citizen A.

19:05.980 --> 19:11.180
We assume there is one objective definition of fair, and someone is just getting it wrong.

19:11.700 --> 19:15.860
But Flyxion's mathematics suggests something entirely different. It suggests they are both

19:15.860 --> 19:20.340
operating with perfect logical consistency within their own internal distinction systems.

19:21.100 --> 19:24.500
The word fair is not a universal constant hovering in the ether.

19:24.760 --> 19:25.760
It's a maintenance burden.

19:25.940 --> 19:30.940
Yes. It is a biological distinction that has been repaired along completely different historical

19:30.940 --> 19:31.520
paths.

19:31.520 --> 19:38.180
For citizen A, maybe their specific life path, you know, the family they grew up in, the economic

19:38.180 --> 19:43.840
struggles they faced, the specific injustices they witnessed, forced their brain to repair the

19:43.840 --> 19:50.060
concept of fairness over and over again until it's stabilized into meaning, say, equal opportunity

19:50.060 --> 19:53.100
and strict proportional reward for individual effort.

19:53.460 --> 19:58.520
Meanwhile, citizen B has a totally different trajectory. Their unique history of encounters,

19:58.520 --> 20:04.240
the historical texts they resonated with, the societal vulnerabilities they observed, forced

20:04.240 --> 20:09.480
their brain to repair fairness to mean the protection of the most vulnerable and the equalization

20:09.480 --> 20:11.340
of baseline survival outcomes.

20:11.620 --> 20:14.700
So when they argue, they aren't actually arguing about the text of the bill at all.

20:14.780 --> 20:15.080
No.

20:15.180 --> 20:19.680
They are smashing their compressed repair histories into each other, which totally reframes why

20:19.680 --> 20:23.460
our modern obsession with fact-checking often feels so utterly useless.

20:23.740 --> 20:28.260
It really does. We have built an entire media ecosystem based on the premise that if we just

20:28.260 --> 20:33.320
throw enough raw, verified data at a disagreement, logic will eventually force the other side

20:33.320 --> 20:33.800
to concede.

20:33.980 --> 20:39.680
But if the disagreement is structural-like, if we are dealing with a path-dependent emergence,

20:40.540 --> 20:46.200
then throwing more facts at the problem is like trying to fix a fundamental software bug

20:46.200 --> 20:49.140
by buying a louder, more expensive computer monitor.

20:49.200 --> 20:51.500
That's a hilarious analogy, but it's spot on.

20:51.500 --> 20:55.840
It doesn't change the underlying code. If the manager and the worker have a structural

20:55.840 --> 21:01.600
gap, the raw facts just get instantly filtered through their misaligned boundaries. The facts

21:01.600 --> 21:06.780
hit the manager's brain and are immediately categorized as proof that the manager is right.

21:06.880 --> 21:07.120
Right.

21:07.560 --> 21:11.440
The exact same facts hit the worker's brain and prove the worker is right.

21:11.440 --> 21:16.260
This is what the paper calls a projection mismatch. When the projections don't align,

21:16.660 --> 21:21.900
no amount of fact-sharing will ever close the gap because the facts are being caught in nets that

21:21.900 --> 21:22.920
are woven differently.

21:23.600 --> 21:28.820
Flyxion explicitly argues that in these moments, we have to stop fact-checking and start translating.

21:29.180 --> 21:31.420
Translation over confrontation. I love that.

21:31.720 --> 21:38.520
Yes. We need negotiation over the boundary itself. What the essay terms joint repair work on the boundary

21:38.520 --> 21:43.600
actually in dispute? You have to stop yelling about the dashboard numbers. You have to pause

21:43.600 --> 21:48.760
and ask, you know, what historical experiences led you to define productivity this way?

21:49.760 --> 21:56.680
Here is the history that shaped my definition. Can we collaboratively build a new shared boundary

21:56.680 --> 21:59.060
that accommodates both of our realities?

21:59.380 --> 22:04.240
It changes the goal from destroying your opponent to co-authoring a new reality.

22:04.440 --> 22:04.920
Exactly.

22:04.920 --> 22:09.520
As I was reading this, I kept thinking, if our whole lives are just patching up these arbitrary

22:09.520 --> 22:14.720
boundaries, repairing our private little mental models over and over, what does it actually mean

22:14.720 --> 22:15.520
to be intelligent?

22:15.640 --> 22:17.100
That's the million-dollar question.

22:17.200 --> 22:21.680
Because we usually measure intelligence by how many facts someone knows. But if facts just sit

22:21.680 --> 22:27.420
inside these fragile patched-up boxes, then a high IQ score doesn't really seem to capture the real

22:27.420 --> 22:28.380
picture, does it?

22:28.380 --> 22:35.540
You've hit on the exact thesis of Section 4 of the paper. Flyxion completely dismantles our standard

22:35.540 --> 22:41.820
culturally accepted definitions of intelligence. The paper argues that our obsession with metrics like

22:41.820 --> 22:48.320
prediction, compression, and optimization are totally insufficient for defining true intelligence.

22:48.540 --> 22:54.120
Let's break those down. Why isn't prediction a good measure of intelligence? I mean, we pay a lot of money

22:54.120 --> 22:55.980
to analysts who can predict the stock market.

22:55.980 --> 23:01.780
Prediction is valuable, absolutely, but it is mechanical. Think about a standard pocket calculator.

23:02.240 --> 23:06.900
A calculator predicts the result of an arithmetic operation with 100% reliability.

23:07.260 --> 23:07.360
Right.

23:07.620 --> 23:13.520
If you type in 500 times 42, it predicts the exact correct answer every single time. But nobody in

23:13.520 --> 23:16.340
their right mind looks at a Casio calculator and calls it intelligent.

23:16.480 --> 23:17.140
No, of course not.

23:17.260 --> 23:22.180
It has no awareness of what it is doing. It is just blindly executing a hard-coded rule.

23:22.180 --> 23:26.720
Okay, that makes sense for a prediction. But what about optimization? Especially right now,

23:26.780 --> 23:32.680
we hear constantly about AI algorithms optimizing for engagement or optimizing complex supply chains.

23:33.380 --> 23:34.720
Optimization feels smart.

23:35.120 --> 23:39.500
Optimization is just a more complex execution. Consider a household thermostat.

23:39.720 --> 23:40.280
A thermostat.

23:40.360 --> 23:45.360
A thermostat optimizes a single variable room temperature continuously and tirelessly.

23:45.360 --> 23:51.020
It takes in sensory data, adjusts the mechanical output, and achieves its goal perfectly.

23:51.400 --> 23:51.480
Yeah.

23:51.580 --> 23:57.040
But a thermostat remains a thermostat. It is not intelligent. What prediction, compression,

23:57.220 --> 24:02.100
and optimization all lack is self-awareness of their own boundaries. They lack the ability

24:02.100 --> 24:06.700
to notice when the fundamental categories they rely on have stopped fitting the real world.

24:06.860 --> 24:10.640
Oh. So affliction separates cognitive action into three distinct tiers.

24:11.040 --> 24:13.220
Execution, competence, and intelligence.

24:13.220 --> 24:17.440
Exactly. Execution is bare application. You have a fixed rule, and you apply it.

24:17.760 --> 24:19.400
The calculator is pure execution.

24:19.480 --> 24:19.900
Got it.

24:20.060 --> 24:24.960
The next tier up is competence. Competence is execution with incredibly low error rates

24:24.960 --> 24:30.580
in ordinary expected situations. A highly trained domain expert operating strictly within a stable,

24:30.820 --> 24:32.820
predictable field is exhibiting competence.

24:33.340 --> 24:40.140
But then we get to the third tier, true intelligence. Infliction defines intelligence strictly as the

24:40.140 --> 24:45.440
capacity to revise the underlying distinction itself when your competence suddenly breaks down.

24:45.560 --> 24:45.960
Yes.

24:46.360 --> 24:50.400
It is the ability to realize you have driven off the edge of your map, and instead of just driving

24:50.400 --> 24:53.700
in circles, you get out a pen and draw a brand new map.

24:53.700 --> 24:58.700
The paper uses a highly relevant modern example for this, the hallucinating AI.

24:59.200 --> 25:00.660
Oh yes, we all know this one.

25:00.920 --> 25:05.540
We interact with these large language models that generate text that sounds incredibly confident,

25:05.540 --> 25:09.760
grammatically flawless, and is factually completely unhinged.

25:09.760 --> 25:13.780
Yeah. It will tell you with absolute authority that the moon is made of string cheese,

25:14.360 --> 25:17.680
and it will actually generate historical citations to prove it.

25:17.920 --> 25:22.640
Flyxion argues this is a system exhibiting high competence with absolutely zero intelligence.

25:22.640 --> 25:28.720
It has fluid operation inside a linguistic frame. It knows probabilistically how words should string

25:28.720 --> 25:34.580
together, but it possesses no internal reflective loop to check if the frame it is using actually

25:34.580 --> 25:36.460
maps to ground truth reality.

25:36.580 --> 25:37.760
It has no idea it's failing.

25:38.140 --> 25:44.520
Right. It cannot notice its own failure pressure. It has execution incompetence, but it entirely

25:44.520 --> 25:48.020
laps the repair capacity that defines genuine intelligence.

25:48.420 --> 25:52.260
This clicked for me when I thought about playing a massive, complex video game.

25:52.260 --> 25:54.280
Let's map the three tiers onto a gamer.

25:54.520 --> 25:55.320
Oh, I like this.

25:55.680 --> 26:00.420
Execution is just knowing the controller, right? The A button jumps, the right trigger shoots.

26:00.680 --> 26:02.760
You can physically execute the commands.

26:03.000 --> 26:03.400
Yes.

26:03.840 --> 26:08.340
Competence is being a genuinely good player. You can beat the levels, you can optimize your speed,

26:08.600 --> 26:14.100
you memorize the enemy attack patterns. You are highly competent inside the boundaries of the

26:14.100 --> 26:14.700
game's rules.

26:14.860 --> 26:16.180
Okay, so what is intelligence?

26:16.860 --> 26:22.120
True intelligence is what happens when a speedrunner finds a level that is fundamentally glitched

26:22.120 --> 26:26.780
and unbeatable by the normal rules. Instead of just pressing the jump button harder, which would

26:26.780 --> 26:29.600
be pure execution, they realize the boundary is broken.

26:29.800 --> 26:30.020
Right.

26:30.140 --> 26:34.820
They figure out how to clip through the wall, manipulate the game's memory code, and invent

26:34.820 --> 26:39.960
a completely new way to interact with the physics engine to bypass the glitch. Intelligence is

26:39.960 --> 26:42.280
repairing the structure of the game so you can keep playing.

26:42.280 --> 26:47.620
That is a brilliant translation of the concept. The speedrunner is exhibiting meta-awareness.

26:47.960 --> 26:52.080
They aren't just playing the game. They are evaluating the rules of the game.

26:52.300 --> 26:52.460
Yeah.

26:52.680 --> 26:58.380
And this leads to a very sobering realization about human intelligence. In this model, intelligence

26:58.380 --> 27:04.620
is not an IQ score. It is not a static number written on a test you took when you were 12.

27:04.760 --> 27:05.380
It's a rate.

27:05.380 --> 27:10.740
Yes. Intelligence is a rate of action. It is a measurement of how fast and how effectively

27:10.740 --> 27:15.660
you are currently updating your distinctions when reality pushes back against you.

27:16.340 --> 27:21.820
I want you, the listener, to do a quick self-audit right now. Have you ever felt yourself actively

27:21.820 --> 27:26.460
losing intelligence in a certain area of your life? Not because you were getting older or

27:26.460 --> 27:30.100
forgetting facts, but because you just got comfortable. You stopped noticing when your

27:30.100 --> 27:32.620
old, reliable knowledge stopped fitting new realities.

27:32.840 --> 27:33.840
That is so common.

27:33.840 --> 27:38.540
Like, maybe you are a master of a specific management style that worked brilliantly in

27:38.540 --> 27:44.680
2015. You were highly competent. But the industry shifted. The workforce changed. The economic

27:44.680 --> 27:45.700
realities updated.

27:45.980 --> 27:52.020
And instead of noticing that friction and actively repairing your approach, you just doubled down.

27:52.220 --> 27:58.100
Exactly. You kept forcing your old competence onto the new reality, wondering why everyone else

27:58.100 --> 27:58.660
was failing.

27:58.660 --> 28:04.620
According to this math, in that exact moment, your intelligence rate dropped to zero. You

28:04.620 --> 28:09.860
became the hallucinating AI. You became the thermostat. You were still functioning mechanically,

28:10.040 --> 28:11.960
but you lost the capacity to repair.

28:12.100 --> 28:18.260
But wait, this brings up a massive danger. Let's say I do notice the problem. I realize my management

28:18.260 --> 28:24.760
style isn't working, or my video game is glitched. Does any fix I make automatically count as intelligence?

28:24.760 --> 28:27.100
That is a very dangerous assumption.

28:27.420 --> 28:32.140
Because I can fix a glitch in a video game by just deleting all the final levels. I solve the

28:32.140 --> 28:34.540
immediate problem, but I ruin the rest of the game.

28:34.660 --> 28:39.940
And that leads us to a critical threshold in the theory. The mathematical danger of successful

28:39.940 --> 28:40.400
fixes.

28:40.620 --> 28:41.640
Successful in quotes, right?

28:41.800 --> 28:48.220
Right. The Klotchen separates repairs into local repair success and global repair success. A locally

28:48.220 --> 28:54.380
successful repair is one that lowers your immediate short-term failure pressure. It solves the crisis right in

28:54.380 --> 28:55.480
front of your face right now.

28:55.660 --> 29:02.300
Like, your boss yells at you about a specific metric being too low, so you patch the reporting software

29:02.300 --> 29:06.680
so that specific metric looks green today. Local success.

29:06.680 --> 29:13.520
But a globally intelligent repair must do something much harder. It must preserve what the math calls

29:13.520 --> 29:15.740
the admissible future region.

29:16.000 --> 29:17.300
Admissible future region.

29:17.400 --> 29:23.740
It has to make the system easier, or at least no harder, to repair tomorrow. A fundamentally bad repair

29:23.740 --> 29:30.380
is one that locally succeeds. The boss stops yelling, but globally fails by radically shrinking

29:30.380 --> 29:31.420
your future option.

29:31.420 --> 29:36.080
Because you hard-coded a fake number into the system to get the green light, and now next quarter,

29:36.480 --> 29:42.000
when the actual raw data is needed for a critical financial audit, the entire database is corrupted,

29:42.280 --> 29:44.180
tangled, and completely unfixable.

29:44.180 --> 29:49.940
Exactly. You traded a minor local annoyance for a catastrophic global failure, and Flyxion

29:49.940 --> 29:54.160
provides an actual rigorous formula for this. Equation 2 in the paper.

29:54.440 --> 29:55.240
Ah, equation 2.

29:55.440 --> 29:59.880
It is the beating heart of the entire framework. It essentially states that intelligence is the

29:59.880 --> 30:01.660
derivative of future repair capacity.

30:01.820 --> 30:05.700
Okay, derivative is a heavy math term. Let's break that down for someone who hasn't taken

30:05.700 --> 30:06.640
calculus in a decade.

30:06.920 --> 30:10.520
Think of a derivative as a rate of change. It's like the steering wheel of a car.

30:10.520 --> 30:16.220
True intelligence isn't just about moving forward. It is about ensuring that the way

30:16.220 --> 30:21.340
you steer right now maximizes your ability to steer away from cliffs in the future.

30:21.740 --> 30:22.080
I see.

30:22.400 --> 30:27.620
Intelligence is fixing things in a way that actively increases your future options for

30:27.620 --> 30:28.580
fixing things.

30:29.040 --> 30:34.180
Let's apply equation 2 to everyday corporate bureaucracy, because I think everyone listening

30:34.180 --> 30:35.400
will recognize this immediately.

30:35.600 --> 30:37.900
Oh, bureaucracy is the perfect test case for this.

30:37.900 --> 30:42.960
Let's imagine an office. One guy, we'll call him Bob, keeps microwaving leftover fish

30:42.960 --> 30:43.860
in the break room at noon.

30:43.900 --> 30:44.980
Oh, classic Bob.

30:45.080 --> 30:49.900
It stings up the entire floor. Everyone hates it. The HR manager is flooded with complaints.

30:50.460 --> 30:57.260
She is experiencing acute failure pressure. So, she implements a repair. She drafts a hyper-specific

30:57.260 --> 31:02.860
massive news section in the employee handbook detailing exactly which species of seafood can and

31:02.860 --> 31:06.900
cannot be heeded in company-owned appliances, complete with time restrictions.

31:06.900 --> 31:11.420
Well, she has achieved local success. The Bob problem is solved. The office smells better

31:11.420 --> 31:11.860
tomorrow.

31:12.300 --> 31:15.620
But based on equation 2, is that an intelligent repair?

31:16.000 --> 31:22.200
It is the textbook definition of an anti-intelligent repair. By adding that massive, hyper-specific

31:22.200 --> 31:28.000
rule, HR solved the immediate bug, but they severely restricted future branch points.

31:28.000 --> 31:28.660
How so?

31:28.980 --> 31:35.340
Now, the employee handbook is thicker. It is more rigid. It requires more legal review. Next month,

31:35.420 --> 31:40.220
when someone microwaves broccoli or burnt popcorn, what do they do? Do they need another subclause?

31:40.360 --> 31:45.980
Right. The bureaucracy calcifies. It becomes this dense thicket of rules built to solve past

31:45.980 --> 31:50.380
local problems, which makes it absolutely impossible to navigate future problems.

31:50.460 --> 31:56.860
Exactly. Infliction uses this exact mathematical dynamic to unify concepts that seem totally unrelated.

31:56.860 --> 31:57.600
Like what?

31:57.960 --> 32:02.900
Think about technical debt in software engineering, where programmers patch bugs with messy code until

32:02.900 --> 32:08.700
the whole system crashes. Think about bureaucratic sclerosis in governments, where ancient laws prevent

32:08.700 --> 32:14.880
new infrastructure. Think about rigid religious dogmatism, where a belief system invents convoluted

32:14.880 --> 32:17.360
rationalizations to ignore scientific evidence.

32:17.460 --> 32:18.860
They are all the exact same phenomena.

32:19.160 --> 32:25.180
They are all instances of successful local repair producing a catastrophic global loss of repairability.

32:25.180 --> 32:30.960
The programmer, the bureaucrat, and the dogmatist are all solving their immediate anxiety, but

32:30.960 --> 32:34.300
they are mathematically driving their system's future into a corner.

32:34.540 --> 32:40.100
That is deeply terrifying. Because we are all doing this. Every single day, we are patching our

32:40.100 --> 32:43.860
lives, our relationships, our work processes with duct tape.

32:44.060 --> 32:45.500
Yeah, we really are.

32:45.500 --> 32:51.600
We feel highly productive because we solved today's crisis, while mathematically, we are driving our

32:51.600 --> 32:57.800
future repair capacity straight into the ground. So how do we stop? If throwing duct tape on the

32:57.800 --> 33:01.220
symptoms is anti-intelligent, how do we perform deep repairs?

33:01.760 --> 33:07.360
To give systems a roadmap for preserving their future, the essay categorizes deep repairs into three major

33:07.360 --> 33:10.620
slogans. CELIO, Tartan, and HITRA.

33:10.620 --> 33:12.900
These sound like secret government projects.

33:13.020 --> 33:17.080
They do. But they are the three extensions of the mathematical architecture that show how to

33:17.080 --> 33:18.600
maintain global admissibility.

33:18.900 --> 33:24.460
Let's start with the first one, CELIO. The slogan here is, repair the projection. What does it actually

33:24.460 --> 33:25.980
mean to have a projection failure?

33:26.480 --> 33:33.760
Flyxion points out a hard truth. We never interact with raw, objective reality directly. Our brains,

33:33.940 --> 33:40.280
our sensors, our data models, they all act as filters. We interact with reality through a projection.

33:40.280 --> 33:41.320
A model.

33:42.220 --> 33:47.720
CLIO failure happens when your specific projection collapses two entirely different things together,

33:47.820 --> 33:52.220
treating them as identical, when the real world actually requires you to keep them apart.

33:52.380 --> 33:58.400
Okay, let me try an analogy here to visualize this. Imagine you were in a dark room trying to sort

33:58.400 --> 34:03.900
three-dimensional objects, but you aren't allowed to touch them. You are only allowed to look at the

34:03.900 --> 34:08.240
two-dimensional shadows they cast on the wall. You see a square shadow, so you create a metal

34:08.240 --> 34:12.780
distinction and label it square. You see a circular shadow, you label it circle. That is your projection

34:12.780 --> 34:16.300
mapping. You are squashing 3D reality into a 2D model.

34:16.380 --> 34:17.380
That is a perfect setup.

34:17.540 --> 34:22.420
But then someone hands you a cylinder. Depending on how the light hits a cylinder, it can cast a square

34:22.420 --> 34:24.980
shadow on the wall, or it can cast a circular shadow.

34:24.980 --> 34:30.840
The physical reality is a cylinder, but your restricted projection forces it into the category of square.

34:31.120 --> 34:37.320
Right. So you label the cylinder as a square, but then the cylinder acts like a cylinder. It rolls off the table.

34:37.700 --> 34:42.100
Real square blocks don't roll. Your label just failed. You experience a disturbance.

34:42.500 --> 34:43.140
And what do you do?

34:43.140 --> 34:49.240
Well, if I try to do a local duct tape repair, I might just invent a new subcategory and say,

34:49.320 --> 34:54.520
ah, this is a special, rare kind of square, a rolling square. I'll just update my spreadsheet.

34:55.060 --> 34:59.940
But Flyxion provides a mathematical proof here called the projection floor. It mathematically

34:59.940 --> 35:05.520
proves that if your base projection is flawed, no amount of label tweaking will ever cure the error.

35:05.620 --> 35:05.900
Never.

35:06.120 --> 35:09.600
Never. If a cylinder looks exactly like a square in 2D,

35:09.600 --> 35:13.820
you can spend a hundred years inventing complex subcategories of squares,

35:14.180 --> 35:18.920
writing longer and longer instruction manuals, but you will never stop being surprised when it

35:18.920 --> 35:25.180
rolls away. Because the problem isn't the label. The problem is the lighting. The only intelligent

35:25.180 --> 35:30.480
repair is to physically walk around the object. You have to step outside the 2D shadow and repair

35:30.480 --> 35:36.420
the projection itself back to 3D. That is the essence of CLIO. You must repair the underlying

35:36.420 --> 35:41.300
machinery generating the distinctions, not just shuffle the distinctions drawn within a broken

35:41.300 --> 35:48.420
machinery. Which brings us to the second slogan, Tartan. The slogan is repair the trajectory. And it

35:48.420 --> 35:52.260
deals with the terrifying concept of invisible trajectory failure.

35:52.460 --> 35:59.040
Invisible failure sounds like a paradox. Like if a system fails, it breaks. How can it be invisible?

35:59.040 --> 36:03.840
It is invisible if your system only measures success state by state, instant by instant.

36:04.380 --> 36:09.800
Tartan demonstrates mathematically that a system can have zero instantaneous loss. It looks perfectly

36:09.800 --> 36:15.220
healthy right now, but the trajectory it is on is drifting irrevocably toward total catastrophic

36:15.220 --> 36:15.820
collapse.

36:15.960 --> 36:18.060
Can you connect this to a real world scenario?

36:18.480 --> 36:24.260
Think of a publicly traded company being run by a CEO obsessed with quarterly earnings. The CEO is

36:24.260 --> 36:29.220
managing strictly state by state. Quarter one, they hit the revenue target, the stock goes up,

36:29.640 --> 36:35.600
zero instant loss. Quarter two, sales dip slightly, so they hit the target by silently slashing the

36:35.600 --> 36:41.340
research and development budget. The spreadsheet looks green, zero instant loss. Quarter three,

36:41.480 --> 36:46.760
they hit the target by deferring critical maintenance on their servers, zero instant loss.

36:46.760 --> 36:53.080
If you are an auditor looking at any single point in time, the company is wildly successful.

36:53.080 --> 36:58.460
But the trajectory they are on the path over time is cannibalizing the foundation. They

36:58.460 --> 37:03.880
are drifting rapidly away from any admissible future. When the collapse finally hits in year

37:03.880 --> 37:08.120
three, the board of directors will act shocked. They will say it was sudden.

37:08.480 --> 37:09.000
It wasn't.

37:09.360 --> 37:13.720
Mathematically, the Tartan failure was a delayed collapse that was happening the entire time.

37:14.240 --> 37:16.840
State level repair is completely blind to it.

37:16.920 --> 37:21.620
Man, that translates so perfectly to personal burnout. Think about the modern worker. You wake up,

37:21.620 --> 37:25.520
you drink three cups of coffee, you go to the office, you hit your deadlines, you come home,

37:25.600 --> 37:27.180
you collapse, you sleep for five hours.

37:27.300 --> 37:28.440
Yeah, that hits close to home.

37:28.580 --> 37:32.820
On Monday, you function perfectly. Your instantaneous loss is zero. On Tuesday,

37:32.920 --> 37:37.280
you do it again, zero loss. You are holding the system together day by day.

37:37.500 --> 37:42.260
But your trajectory is a slow, silent drift away from biological health.

37:42.720 --> 37:47.760
